# rabbits — what does a hunter actually do to a population?
#
# The smallest model that asks one question. Rabbits breed; hunters take some
# off. Nothing else. No grass, no old age, no winter — those are deliberately
# not here, and the model will tell you where they were missed.
#
#     burrow ──> RABBITS ──> larder
#                  ^  \        ^
#                  |   \       |
#                (reads)     (reads hunters)
#
# TWO KINDS OF ARROW, and the whole model turns on the difference:
#
#   FLOW      solid.  Something moves. burrow -> rabbits is birth; rabbits ->
#             larder is a kill. Mass leaves one end and arrives at the other.
#   INFLUENCE dashed. Nothing moves; a number sets a speed. `hunters` never
#             becomes a rabbit — it only sets how fast the kill flow runs. That
#             is what `by = hunters` says, and why hunters has no edges at all.
#
# hunters is a HELD node — no flow in or out, it just sits at its level and gets
# read (the glucose dial in mito-fuel). Drag it. That IS the experiment.
#
# k = 0.01 is one hunter's EFFECTIVENESS, and it is doing a lot of work: shots
# missed, ground not walked, rabbits that got to the hedge first, the days
# nobody went out. All of it collapses into one number, because the engine is
# deterministic — it gives you the average season, never an unlucky Tuesday.
#
# WHAT TO LOOK FOR — the answer is sharper than it has any right to be.
#   births = 0.30 · rabbits          kills = 0.01 · rabbits · hunters
#   net    = rabbits · (0.30 − 0.01 · hunters)
# `rabbits` factors straight out. So the sign of the net flow does not depend on
# how many rabbits there are — only on the hunters:
#
#   hunters < 30   the population grows, forever, no matter how few rabbits
#   hunters > 30   the population dies, all the way to zero, no matter how many
#   hunters = 30   births and kills cancel — it sits wherever you started it
#
# Try 29, then 31. Not a dent and a bigger dent: growth and extinction. There is
# no hunter count that holds the rabbits at a comfortable level — the knife edge
# is a single value, and it is nowhere near the middle of the dial.
#
# WHERE THE MISSING PIECES SHOW UP:
#   - Set hunters to 0 and the rabbits pin at range(0, 1000). That ceiling is
#     the clamp, not biology. There is no grass in this model, so nothing but
#     the range stops them — the model is reporting what was left out.
#   - Push hunters high and the kill rate climbs without limit: at 800 rabbits,
#     30 hunters take 240 a day. A hunter has daylight and two hands. Real
#     predation SATURATES, and `product` cannot say so — hill(...) reads only
#     one node, so it cannot saturate in rabbits AND scale with hunters. That is
#     a genuine gap in the language, and this is the model that found it.

node rabbits : range(0, 1000), init(200) @ bucket(fill = dots)
node hunters : range(0, 60),   init(20)  @ bucket(fill = dots)   # the dial — no flows, read only

external burrow    # birth: new rabbits, limited by nothing in this model
external larder    # the one sink — every rabbit that leaves, leaves this way

burrow  -> rabbits : proportional(of = rabbits, k = 0.30)              # breeding reads the stock it fills
rabbits -> larder  : product(of = rabbits, by = hunters, k = 0.01)     # kill = effectiveness · rabbits · hunters
